      "title": "Postfix Calculator",
      "topics": [],
      "fastest": true,
      "reverse": false,
      "shortest": true,
      "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    char expression[2002];\n    int stack[100];\n    int count = 0;\n\n    if (fgets(expression, sizeof(expression), stdin) == NULL) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    for (char *token = strtok(expression, \" \\r\\n\");\n         token != NULL;\n         token = strtok(NULL, \" \\r\\n\")) {\n        if (token[1] != '\\0' || strchr(\"+-*\", token[0]) == NULL) {\n            stack[count++] = (int)strtol(token, NULL, 10);\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        int right = stack[--count];\n        int left = stack[--count];\n        int result = 0;\n\n        switch (token[0]) {\n            case '+': result = left + right; break;\n            case '-': result = left - right; break;\n            case '*': result = left * right; break;\n        }\n\n        stack[count++] = result;\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", stack[0]);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "statement": "Evaluate a valid <<postfix expression>> containing integers and the operators {{+}}, {{-}} and {{*}}. Print its result.\n\nIn postfix notation, an operator comes after its two operands. Read from left to right: keep each integer, and when you meet an operator, replace the two most recently kept values with their result. The earlier value is the left operand and the later value is the right operand. For example, {{10 4 -}} means {{10 - 4}}, giving {{6}}. Negative integers such as {{-3}} are single tokens.\n\n<<Example:>>\n{{3 5 + 10 4 - *}}\nFirst, {{3 5 +}} becomes {{8}}: {{8 10 4 - *}}.\nNext, {{10 4 -}} becomes {{6}}: {{8 6 *}}.\nFinally, {{8 6 *}} becomes {{48}}, so print {{48}}.",
